---
import { getCollection, getEntry } from "astro:content";
import AlgebraWindow from "../components/AlgebraWindow.astro";
import DescentFigure from "../components/DescentFigure.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { hyphenated, smart } from "../lib/typography.ts";

const page = await getEntry("pages", "home");
if (!page) throw new Error("Missing home content entry");
const { title, description, headline, lede } = page.data;
const notes = (await getCollection("notes")).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.published.valueOf() - a.data.published.valueOf(),
);
const date = (d: Date) =>
  new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-US", { year: "numeric", month: "long", timeZone: "UTC" }).format(d);
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/" currentPath="/">
  <div class="column">
    <h1 class="hero-title">
      {
        hyphenated(headline ?? "").map((part) =>
          part.keep ? <span class="nowrap">{part.text}</span> : part.text,
        )
      }<span class="hour-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sr-only">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">{lede}</p>
    <p class="note">
      AMLO is a research prototype. Its foundation software exists. The central claim, that
      research run this way picks designs that hold at full size for less compute, is not yet
      shown. <a href="/lab/#status">What is built</a>
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/preview/">Arrange a conversation</a>
      <a href="/lab/">Inside AMLO</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="column">
    <h2 id="models">How AMLO sees a model</h2>
    <p>
      AMLO writes a model as a typed term, in which every part is a definition. The drawing shows
      two models written this way, each inside the training step that trains it. Monad is a small
      language model, and the second is a toy version of DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash. The parameter counts
      in the captions are computed from the definitions. Select a box to open it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <AlgebraWindow />

  <div class="column">
    <h2 id="scale">From 3,500 years to one hour</h2>
    <p>
      On one chip, the 30.84 million GPU-hours of Llama 3.1 405B would take about 3,500 years (<a
        href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-405B">model card</a
      >). Each square in the drawing holds a tenth of the hours of the one before, down to the
      single hour that trains a model of 100 million parameters. For each step, the table gives the
      model those hours can train and the time one chip would need.
    </p>
  </div>

  <DescentFigure />

  <div class="column">
    <p>
      A question that needs a run of the first kind is asked a few times a year, by a few
      organizations. A question that needs an hour can be asked a hundred times a week on one GPU,
      by a university lab, a startup or a single researcher. More ideas get a test. Designs that
      learn more from less data, use less energy or behave more predictably are found sooner, and
      by more people. The cost of more capable AI then turns less on who can pay for the most runs,
      and more on who asks the best questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The arithmetic is the easy part. A small experiment is cheap, but knowing when its answer
      holds at full size is hard. AMLO is being built for that part. There is precedent. Yang et
      al. tuned a proxy of 40 million parameters and moved its hyperparameters to a 6.7B GPT-3
      model, which then beat the published 6.7B results, for a tuning cost of 7% of pretraining (<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.03466">Tensor Programs V</a
      >). That is published transfer of hyperparameters, not an AMLO result and not transfer of an
      architecture.
    </p>

    <h2 id="problem">Why small experiments mislead</h2>
    <p>
      Small-scale evidence fails in known ways. A design that trained a little longer, or drew a
      better learning rate, looks like a better design. The best of a hundred noisy scores is
      biased upward, because the noise helped to choose it. Small and large models can also train
      differently, so a ranking found at one size need not hold at the other (<a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.14522">Yang and Hu</a
      >). In one study of architecture search, random search with early stopping did as well as
      leading methods (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.07638">Li and Talwalkar</a>). In
      another, the training protocol explained more of the reported gains than the searched
      architectures did (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.12522">Yang, Esperança and Carlucci</a
      >).
    </p>
    <p>
      A result like this is a false green. It looks like progress because the evidence that would
      refute it was never collected. A careful researcher catches some false greens, but that care
      is hard to repeat, hard to audit, and does not grow with the number of experiments.
    </p>

    <h2 id="standard">The standard</h2>
    <p>
      Cheap evidence saves money only if it can be trusted. AMLO is being built to hold every
      result to one standard, whatever the outcome.
    </p>
    <dl class="defs">
      <dt>Declared first</dt>
      <dd>
        The question, what changes and what stays fixed are written down before anything runs.
      </dd>
      <dt>Equal footing</dt>
      <dd>
        Every design in a comparison gets the same resources, and the record shows what each one
        used.
      </dd>
      <dt>Earned scale</dt>
      <dd>A result found on small models counts only after it holds on larger ones.</dd>
      <dt>Unseen data</dt>
      <dd>The final check uses data that the search never saw.</dd>
      <dt>Honest outcomes</dt>
      <dd>
        Every result is supported, inconclusive or refuted. An inconclusive result is kept, not
        hidden.
      </dd>
      <dt>A full record</dt>
      <dd>
        Each decision records what was compared, under what resources, and why it passed or failed.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      A language model can propose a change, but it never decides what passes. Rules fixed before
      the run decide that. <a href="/lab/#method">Inside AMLO</a> sets out how a study runs, with its
      tiers and statistics, and shows a simulated claim record.
    </p>

    <h2 id="savings">What it saves</h2>
    <dl class="defs">
      <dt>Compute</dt>
      <dd>
        Most designs are judged on small models and never trained large. A full-size run goes to a
        design that has already passed every cheaper test.
      </dd>
      <dt>Labor</dt>
      <dd>
        The system runs the comparisons, applies the checks and writes the record. Researchers set
        the question and review the decisions.
      </dd>
      <dt>Time</dt>
      <dd>
        Many small experiments fit in the time of one large run, and a weak design stops after a
        cheap test, not after weeks at full size.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      These are the savings the system is built to deliver, not yet measured ones. Measuring them
      against current practice, including other proxy-based search methods, is a study of its own.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stages">Where it goes</h2>
    <p>
      The first application is choosing which architecture to train, before a team commits a large
      training budget. Architecture is the first object of the search, not the last. The design
      widens it in three stages, with the same standard at each. After the architecture comes the
      program that trains it, with its data mixtures and their schedules, objectives, optimizers
      and phases of training. Then comes the deployed system, with its tools, memory, retrieval,
      verifiers and the inference resources it can spend.
    </p>
    <p>
      The long-term aim goes one step further, to a system that applies the same standard to its
      own method and keeps a change to how it searches only when the evidence supports it. That is
      a research aim, not a stage of the current plan, and it needs evidence of its own.{" "}
      <a href="/research/three-expansions/">Read the program note on the three stages</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
    <ul class="doc-list">
      {
        notes.map((note) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/research/${note.id}/`}>
              <span class="doc-title">{note.data.title}</span>
              <span class="doc-meta">
                {note.data.status} · {date(note.data.published)}
              </span>
              <span class="doc-summary">{smart(note.data.description)}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="founder">Who is building it</h2>
    <p>
      Niru ML is an independent research initiative led by Brendon Reperttang, who graduated from
      MIT in Computer Science and Engineering in 2024. AMLO is private, and access is by
      introduction. Researchers, labs, investors and partners can{" "}
      <a href="/preview/">arrange a conversation</a> and see the foundation work.{" "}
      <a href="/about/">About Niru and its founder</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
